<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Coenwulf of Mercia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 796–821</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Coenwulf was king of Mercia from 796 to 821, a distant kinsman of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/offa-of-mercia">Offa</a>. He crushed a revolt in Kent in 798, captured its leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eadberht-praen">Eadberht Præn</a> and later made his own brother king there, keeping Mercia the strongest of the English kingdoms. His long quarrel with Archbishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wulfred">Wulfred of Canterbury</a> over church lands troubled his last years.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/coenwulf-of-mercia.jpg" alt="Coenwulf of Mercia" width="320" height="331" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Scan: British Museum · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coenwulf,_King_of_Mercia,_gold_mancus;_struck_796%E2%80%93821_AD_(obverse).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
